.he "make (1)                  9/21/26                      make (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
make
- maintain program groups
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
make [ -f makefile ] [ option ] ... [ name ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Make executes commands in a description file to bring one or more
target names up to date.  A target is remade if it does not exist, or
if any file it depends on has been modified since the target was last
modified.
.sp
If no -f option is given, "makefile" and then "Makefile" are tried in
order; a description of "-" means the standard input.
.sp
If no name arguments are given, make builds the first target named in
the description file that does not begin with a dot or that contains a
slash.  A name argument of the form name=value defines a macro before
the description file is read and cannot be redefined there.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
INPUT LANGUAGE
.in +5
.br
A description file holds a sequence of entries.  The first line of an
entry is a blank-separated list of targets, a colon, and a list of
prerequisite files.  Text after a semicolon, and every following line
that begins with a tab, is a shell command run to update the target.
Lines beginning with # are comments.  A \ at the end of a line joins it
to the next; the backslash and newline become a blank.
.sp
A rule takes the form
.nf
	target ... : prerequisite ... [; command]
		command
		command
.fi
.sp
Several targets may share one rule, in which case each is considered
separately.  A prerequisite may be another target in the file, whose
own rule is then run first; a prerequisite that names no target in the
file is simply a file.  A rule with a single colon collects all the
prerequisites of every rule for that target into one list, and only
one list of commands is taken.  A rule with a double colon (::) is
independent: each such rule for a target is examined and, if need be,
run on its own.
.sp
A prerequisite containing *, ? or [ is expanded against the directory,
so "*.o" names every .o file.  This happens before the rule is used,
so newly made files are not included.
.sp
Entries of the form "string = value" define a macro; later $(string),
${string}, or $s for a single character, is replaced by its value.
Macros may refer to other macros.  A $$ is a literal $.  Macro values
are substituted on rule lines as the lines are read, and into the
commands just before they are run.  A definition on the make command
line, as in "make CC=ccc", overrides the description file.
.sp
The characters $*, $@, $< and $? are replaced as follows.
.nf
	$@	the target name
	$*	the target name with its last . extension removed
	$<	the prerequisite source of an implicit rule
	$?	the out-of-date prerequisites, blank separated
.fi
.sp
$@ and $* are also expanded in a prerequisite, so "obj.o: $*.c" makes
obj.o depend on obj.c.  $< is set only for suffix rules and for the
.DEFAULT rule; $* is set only for suffix rules.
.sp
A suffix rule names a transformation between two suffixes.  The entry
.nf
	.SUFFIXES : .c .o
	.c.o :
		ccc -O -c $<
.fi
.sp
says how a .o file is made from a .c file: a target x.o whose source
x.c exists is remade with the commands of the .c.o rule, with $< set
to x.c, $* to x, and $@ to x.o.  Both suffixes must appear in the
.SUFFIXES list.  If x.c does not exist, make looks for a further rule
to make it, so chains of suffix rules work.
.sp
The built-in suffix list is
.nf
	.out .o .c .f .e .r .y .yr .ye .l .s
.fi
.sp
with rules for .c.o, .s.o, .c.out, .s.out, .o.out and the yacc, lex
and f77 suffixes, among others.  The built-in macros are CC=ccc,
AS=asz, CFLAGS=, LOADLIBES= and the like.  The -r option suppresses
the built-in rules and macros; a .SUFFIXES entry with no dependents
clears the list.
.sp
A command that begins with @ is not echoed.  A command that begins
with - is run and its nonzero exit ignored.  The two may be combined,
in either order.  A command with no shell metacharacter is run
directly; otherwise it is given to sh -c.
.sp
Several names are special.  .DEFAULT supplies commands for a target
that has no rule of its own; $< is then the target name.  .IGNORE and
.SILENT act like the -i and -s options.  .PRECIOUS lists files that
are not removed when make is interrupted.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-d	Print debugging information.

.ti -10
-p	Print the dependency graph and the macros.

.ti -10
-s	Silent mode; do not print commands before running them.

.ti -10
-i	Ignore nonzero exit codes (as the .IGNORE entry).

.ti -10
-k	On a failure, abandon the current entry but continue
branches that do not depend on it.

.ti -10
-S	Stop at the first failed command (the default; it
overrides an earlier -k).

.ti -10
-n	Print, but do not run, the commands.

.ti -10
-t	Touch the targets (update their times) instead of running
commands.

.ti -10
-q	Do nothing; exit 0 if the target is up to date, -1 if not.

.ti -10
-r	Start with no built-in suffix rules or macros.

.ti -10
-f name	Read the description file name; - is the standard input.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
To build prog from prog.o and util.o, using the built-in rules to make
the .o files from their .c files:
.nf
	prog: prog.o util.o
		ccc -O -o prog prog.o util.o
.fi
.sp
The same, with macros and an explicit suffix rule:
.nf
	CC = ccc
	CFLAGS = -O

	prog: prog.o util.o
		$(CC) $(CFLAGS) -o $@ prog.o util.o

	.c.o:
		$(CC) $(CFLAGS) -c $<
.fi
.sp
A lex source turned into an object with its own suffix rule, showing
$< and $@:
.nf
	.SUFFIXES: .l .o

	.l.o:
		lex $<
		ccc -O -c lex.yy.c
		mv lex.yy.o $@
.fi
.sp
A target with two independent groups of commands: each group runs in
turn, which a single-colon rule would not allow:
.nf
	all::
		ccc -O -o prog prog.o

	all::
		ccc -O -o test test.o
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Archive members a(b) are dated by the archive as a whole: micronix's
object format is not a.out, so a member's own timestamp is not read.
.sp
Make stops at the first command that fails; use -k to continue with
independent targets.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ccc (1), sh (1), asz (1), lex (1)
